  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/1606.02553v1</id>
    <title>The Oslo model, hyperuniformity, and the quenched Edwards-Wilkinson model</title>
    <updated>2016-06-08T13:35:08Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1606.02553v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/1606.02553v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>We present simulations of the 1-dimensional Oslo rice pile model in which the critical height at each site is randomly reset after each toppling. We use the fact that the stationary state of this sandpile model is hyperuniform to reach system of sizes $&gt; 10^7$. Most previous simulations were seriously flawed by important finite size corrections. We find that all critical exponents have values consistent with simple rationals: $ν=4/3$ for the correlation length exponent, $D =9/4$ for the fractal dimension of avalanche clusters, and $z=10/7 $ for the dynamical exponent. In addition we relate the hyperuniformity exponent to the correlation length exponent $ν$. Finally we discuss the relationship with the quenched Edwards-Wilkinson (qEW) model, where we find in particular that the local roughness exponent is $α_{\rm loc} = 1$.</summary>
    <category term="cond-mat.stat-mech" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2016-06-08T13:35:08Z</published>
